WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.700
Many scripts use arguments to select a mode, target, environment, region, package, service, or action.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.360
These values should rarely be open-ended.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.820
A deployment script should know which environments are supported.

4
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.360
A maintenance script should know which modes are valid.

5
00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.900
A service script should know which service identifiers it can operate on.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.280
Allowlists keep validation close to the script's real intent.

7
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:32.700
A collection of individually plausible arguments can form an unsupported workflow when combinations are not validated.

8
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:37.300
A useful pattern is to map user-facing choices to known internal behavior.

9
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:43.240
The caller might request a supported environment name, and the script maps that name to a controlled configuration.

10
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:51.820
The caller should not be able to provide arbitrary operational control through a free-form argument when the workflow only supports a small set of safe choices.

11
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.180
Argument combinations also matter.

12
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.160
Some options may require other options.

13
00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.260
Some should never appear together.

14
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.640
Some modes may support only certain targets.

15
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:08.180
A defensive script validates the whole request, not just each individual value in isolation.

16
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.560
This prevents a collection of individually plausible arguments from forming an unsupported workflow.

17
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.360
Unsupported values should fail before action begins.

18
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:23.220
Rejection messages should be clear enough to correct the request without exposing sensitive internals.

19
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:30.360
When scripts validate modes, targets, and combinations early, they become easier to operate and easier to review.

20
00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:37.420
The script's accepted command line becomes an explicit contract rather than an accidental side effect of branching logic.
